+++ b/docs/real-services.md
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@ This section is for someone who wants to try the desk against their own free Hub
 `scripts/run_real_check.py` runs the same desk against a real CRM (a free HubSpot account) and real e-mail (a mail account reached over an encrypted connection), then COUNTS what really happened. Only the INPUT is made up: about 12 invented people whose addresses end in `example.com` (a domain name reserved for examples); the mail kind used here picks the receiving address only from your configured inbox list, never from a lead's address (`tests/test_smtp_provider.py`, test of the recipient choice). You run it from your own terminal; it asks "y/N" after printing the worst-case number of calls and mails (and dollars, only with `--live-model`), and it refuses to start when a required cap or setting is missing (`tests/test_real_check_dry.py`, test of a missing required name).
 
 1. Copy `scripts/real-run.env.example` to a folder outside the repository and fill it in. Each name is explained inside the file. Secrets are never in that file, only the paths of one-line key files (a HubSpot private-app token with contacts read and write only, called the service key in the config file; the desk does not use HubSpot's owners list, only the probe does; and a mail app password).
+   A key file is checked by `scripts/run_real_check.py` before the question (a bad one stops the run before any cost line, any reservation and any connection), and then read AGAIN by each program that uses it (the desk, the probe, the model stand-in). It is not read once and handed over: a file that you change, replace or damage between the check and that later read is refused by the program that reads it, with the same one-line message that names the setting only and never the value. Nothing is cached between the two reads.
 1a. **Before turning lead stages on in any real HubSpot account (required; the desk then writes HubSpot's lead status field):** the CRM admin lists every automation that reacts to lead status changes and confirms each one is safe or switched off; test on one made-up contact first. The desk writes the lead status field and cannot see those automations (`docs/limits.md`, section "Which CRM stage things the desk touches").
 2. In HubSpot create the contact property `hadavin_lead_id` (single-line text) by hand; the program's HubSpot code uses only the contacts endpoints and creates no property (`src/lead_desk/crm_hubspot.py`, top comment; no test checks what the real HubSpot does when the property is missing: the tests run the code against a demo server that accepts the field anyway and answers every address except the contact addresses with 404, and the create test in `tests/test_crm_hubspot.py` counts one call for one create).
 3. Self-test first, no real service: `python scripts/run_real_check.py --dry-fakes`. Then the real run: `python scripts/run_real_check.py --config PATH`. Options: `--live-model` (a real AI model; its dollar limit is set in the settings whose names start with `DESK_LIVE_`), `--cleanup` (archives only the contacts this run made, found by their lead ids).
